WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.367
Serving and cost extras.

2
00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:07.733
Eleven more names beyond vLLM and LiteLLM, from engines to routing.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:13.367
SGLang is the serving engine challenging vLLM on throughput.

4
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:16.467
Its secret is RadixAttention, a prefix tree.

5
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:19.700
Requests with a common prefix share their KV cache.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.667
That is excellent for agent workloads full of repeated prompts.

7
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:28.833
TGI is Hugging Face's Text Generation Inference server.

8
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:33.500
It is Rust-based, production-hardened, and powers Hugging Face endpoints.

9
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.467
It is the third name, with vLLM and SGLang, in every serving job description.

10
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:45.633
TensorRT-LLM is NVIDIA's LLM-specific compiler and runtime.

11
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:52.867
It uses fused kernels, FP8, and in-flight batching to reach peak performance on NVIDIA GPUs.

12
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:57.258
The cost is a build step for every model and GPU combination.

13
00:00:57.258 --> 00:00:58.667
It pairs with Triton.

14
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:03.300
LMDeploy is the InternLM team's serving toolkit.

15
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.533
Its TurboMind engine delivers very fast quantized serving.

16
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.900
It is a name you will often see in benchmark tables.

17
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.400
These are cross-platform inference runtimes.

18
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.167
Start with your trained model.

19
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.733
Export it to ONNX.

20
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:20.200
ONNX Runtime then runs it anywhere.

21
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.416
And OpenVINO optimizes it for Intel CPUs and NPUs.

22
00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:28.567
This is the classical model and edge deployment path.

23
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:33.500
These are model-serving frameworks, a level above raw engines.

24
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.967
BentoML packages any model as a container with APIs.

25
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:41.667
Ray Serve composes scalable inference graphs in Python.

26
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:48.567
Medusa and lookahead decoding are cousins of speculative decoding that skip the draft model.

27
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:52.721
Medusa adds extra decoding heads to guess several tokens.

28
00:01:52.721 --> 00:01:55.900
Lookahead generates n-gram guesses in parallel.

29
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.700
Same goal: more tokens per forward pass.

30
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.967
Dynamic and in-flight batching sound alike, but they are different schemes.

31
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.623
Dynamic batching is Triton's older, request-level scheme.

32
00:02:08.623 --> 00:02:11.833
In-flight batching is TensorRT-LLM's term.

33
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:16.352
Dynamic batching groups requests that arrive within a short window.

34
00:02:16.352 --> 00:02:21.200
In-flight batching admits new requests at each decode step, as others finish.

35
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.567
So in-flight batching is TensorRT-LLM's name for continuous batching, which you met in vLLM.

36
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:34.133
GPTCache is the open source library for semantic caching.

37
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:35.600
A query arrives.

38
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:36.767
We embed it.

39
00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:39.200
We look for a near-duplicate in the cache.

40
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.187
If one is found, we serve the cached response.

41
00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:46.100
You can roll your own with Redis and embeddings, but know the name.

42
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.400
Model routing sends each query to the cheapest model that can handle it.

43
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.500
Every query is judged first.

44
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:59.567
Simple ones go to a small model like Haiku or mini, and complex ones go to a frontier model.

45
00:02:59.867 --> 00:03:05.317
You can implement it with LiteLLM, Portkey, OpenRouter, or your own classifier.

46
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.800
It is the single biggest cost lever.

47
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.233
Portkey and OpenRouter are managed AI gateways.

48
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:18.900
One API sits over all providers, with routing, fallbacks, caching, budgets, and observability.

49
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.300
Portkey targets enterprises, while OpenRouter aggregates models for developers.

50
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.624
Let's recap.

51
00:03:26.624 --> 00:03:33.367
SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, and LMDeploy are the serving engines to know.

52
00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:39.133
ONNX, OpenVINO, BentoML, and Ray Serve help you deploy beyond raw engines.

53
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:42.733
Medusa and in-flight batching squeeze out more throughput.

54
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:46.567
And caching, routing, and gateways are your biggest cost levers.
